<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pratinas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 500 BCE</span> · Also known as Pratinas of Phlius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pratinas of Phlius was a Greek dramatist and lyric poet active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> around 500 BCE, when he competed against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>. Ancient writers credited him with introducing the satyr play, the comic drama with a chorus of satyrs that followed each set of tragedies. Only a fragment of one of his choral songs survives, and his son Aristias was also a dramatist.</p></div>
